{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1087", "sample_idx": 47, "model_name": "meta/llama-4-maverick-17b-128e-instruct", "content": "the social network is a fascinating film that delves into the origins of facebook, the social media giant that has revolutionized the way people interact with each other. directed by david fincher, the movie is a riveting drama that explores the complexities of ambition, friendship, and the consequences of success.\n\nthe film begins with mark zuckerberg (played by jesse eisenberg), a brilliant and introverted harvard student, creating a website called \"facemash\" that allows users to compare the photos of two students and vote on which one is more attractive. the site becomes an instant hit, but also generates a lot of controversy, leading to mark's disciplinary hearing.\n\nas the story unfolds, we see mark team up with his friends eduardo savernin (andrew garfield), dustin moskovitz (josh pence), and chris hughes (patrick mapel) to create a new website called \"thefacebook\". the site quickly gains popularity, and mark becomes increasingly obsessed with its success, often at the expense of his relationships with his friends.\n\none of the most striking aspects of the film is its portrayal of mark zuckerberg as a complex and multifaceted character. jesse eisenberg's performance is nuanced and captivating, conveying mark's intelligence, ambition, and social awkwardness. we see mark as a genius who is driven by a desire to prove himself, but also as someone who is emotionally stunted and struggles to form meaningful connections with others.\n\nthe film also explores the legal battles that ensued as facebook grew in popularity, with mark being sued by the twins cameron and tyler winklevoss (armie hammer), who claim that mark stole their idea for a social networking site. the scenes depicting the deposition are particularly tense and dramatic, with mark coming across as dismissive and arrogant.\n\nthroughout the film, fincher's direction is masterful, using a range of techniques to create a sense of tension and unease. the cinematography is sleek and modern, with a predominantly dark color palette that reflects the characters' emotional states. the score by trent reznor and atticus ross is also noteworthy, adding to the film's sense of unease and foreboding.\n\none of the themes that emerges from the film is the corrosive effect of ambition on personal relationships. as mark becomes increasingly focused on facebook's success, he begins to sacrifice his friendships and his integrity. the film suggests that the pursuit of success can be a lonely and isolating experience, and that the costs of achieving greatness can be high.\n\noverall, the social network is a thought-provoking and engaging film that offers a fascinating glimpse into the origins of facebook and the complex personalities involved. it's a movie that will appeal to anyone interested in technology, entrepreneurship, or the human drama that underlies the world of social media.", "logprobs": [{"token": "the", "logprob": -0.9773347973823547}, {"token": " social", "logprob": -7.30726242181845e-05}, {"token": " network", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " is", "logprob": -0.014163684099912643}, {"token": " a", "logprob": -9.536738616588991e-07}, {"token": " fascinating", "logprob": -1.9877774715423584}, {"token": " film", "logprob": -0.018251920118927956}, {"token": " that", "logprob": -0.00033742457162588835}, {"token": " delves", "logprob": -0.6183348298072815}, {"token": " into", "logprob": -4.541770613286644e-05}, {"token": " the", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " origins", "logprob": -1.073547124862671}, {"token": " of", "logprob": -2.1457441107486375e-05}, {"token": " facebook", "logprob": -0.00441255746409297}, {"token": ",", "logprob": -0.04858732968568802}, {"token": " the", "logprob": -0.011069082655012608}, {"token": " social", "logprob": -0.0016689670737832785}, {"token": " media", "logprob": -3.576278118089249e-07}, {"token": " giant", "logpr